        "message_id": "message_c380b71d53b02daa4eb70c37",
        "role": "user"
      },
      {
        "content": "The big picture is this. First, the buyback means the manufacturer takes the truck back and refunds what you paid toward it. That includes things like finance charges, sales tax, license fees, the initial registration fee if it’s on the contract, and other official fees tied to getting it.\n\nSecond, the manufacturer pays our fees and costs, not you. You never get a bill from us. The hourly rates in the agreement are just there so you can see how those fees are calculated.\n\nThird, the only percentage in there is on additional damages, meaning money above the buyback. So, for the sake of argument, if the buyback were fifty thousand and the case settled for a hundred thousand, the first fifty is all yours, our fees are paid separately by the manufacturer, and the extra fifty gets split fifty-fifty. In that example, you’d walk away with seventy-five thousand, which is a hundred and fifty percent of what you paid.\n\nFourth, there can be a mileage offset on the buyback. That’s written into the lemon law itself, it’s based on the miles driven before the problem first showed up, and it’s fixed, so it doesn’t keep growing while the case is pending.\n\nThat’s the whole thing in a nutshell. What questions can I answer before I walk you through signing?",
        "created_timestamp": 1792352915424,
        "message_id": "message_96ff1ea4404f0c5c405a2f31",
        "metadata": {
          "response_id": 2
